{"t":1792740658,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"fc9d93bb-fa1d-46fe-8c74-f733fb187e01","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-23T05:53:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 230553\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n153 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast thinking overnight, but \ndid add/fine tune the mention of frost the next couple nights.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in many areas \n  tonight and again Saturday night.\n\n- 2) Dry and seasonable conditions are likely to continue\n  through much of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in \nmany areas tonight and again Saturday night.\n\nWith a large area of Canadian high pressure building overhead\nthe next 24 to 48 hours, conditions will likely become favorable\nfor radiational cooling (light to calm winds, mainly clear skies\nand a cool background airmass). The coldest conditions are\nexpected through early Sunday morning, with some moderation in\ntemperatures thereafter.\n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nFor tonight, temperatures look to fall into the mid 30s for many\nareas west of I-95, perhaps even into interior/rural southern MD.\nThe typically colder spots of northern/north-central MD and the\ncentral VA piedmont could at least briefly come close to freezing.\nWill see what the latest guidance and trends have later this\nmorning before making any headline decisions.\n\nFor Saturday night, more widespread cold (possible frost/freeze)\nconditions are apparent as high pressure settles more directly\noverhead. This would bring the frost/freeze threat for most\nareas outside the major urban centers and immediate shore of the\nChesapeake Bay. If the colder temperature guidance verifies,\nthen the growing season may end for all areas west I-95 come\nearly Sunday morning.\n\nRelied on a blend of MOS guidance and NBM 10th percentile for \nlow temperatures the next couple of nights, even undercutting \nby a couple degrees in the typically colder locales.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry and seasonable conditions are likely to \ncontinue through much of next week.\n\nSprawling Canadian high pressure will build into the Northeast\nand Mid-Atlantic through the weekend as ridging in the upper\nlevels slowly builds in from the west. Most of the time should\nbe void of many if any clouds, though some high clouds are\npossible as a weakening remnant upper trough pivots and pulls\naway through tonight. Thereafter, there are some hints at just\nenough saturation in the low levels for perhaps some patchy low\nclouds mainly during the early morning hours by later in the\nweekend. No precipitation is expected, however.\n\nHeading into early next week, a backdoor front looks to drop\ninto the region from the north. This will be a dry frontal\npassage with not much noticeable change in airmass as it stalls\nand weakens over the area. In fact, temperatures may trend\nmilder early next week as ridging continues to build aloft.\n\nBy mid to late next week, there are some hints that upper\nridging begins to break down. Surface high pressure is expected\nto hold firm through the forecast period, but there may be a\nglimmer of (very) low precipitation chances beyond that point.\n\nWith the rather persistently dry airmass in place, the latest\nforecast package leans on lower/drier dew point guidance that\nhas been verifying better of late, especially for the next \ncouple of days as the pattern remains largely unchanged.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through early next week.\nThere are some hints that a slight uptick in low-level moisture\ncould lead to some patchy fog or low clouds mainly during the\nearly morning hours by later in the weekend, though confidence\nin timing and extent of sub-VFR is low at this time.\n\nN winds with a few gusts to around 15 kts are expected just \nafter daybreak until roughly 15Z, becoming NE this afternoon. \nWinds will be light to calm tonight. A more E flow is likely\nSaturday, with light to calm winds again Saturday night. Wind\ndirection may be a bit variable, ranging from SE to NE as high\npressure passes directly overhead Sunday. Light SE winds early\nMonday become NW by midday Monday, then N with a few gusts\npossible Tuesday as a backdoor cold front drops through.\n\nIt is possible that the first near-freezing temperatures of the\nseason are observed particularly early Sunday morning for sites\nlike CHO, IAD and BWI.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNortherly flow will remain gusty through this morning for most\narea waterways (excluding the middle/upper tidal Potomac River).\nWinds will gradually subside through the day, likely becoming\nsub-SCA across all waters by this evening. Some hi-res guidance\nindicates the potential for (1) brief near SCA gusts over the\nmid/upper tidal Potomac River around mid morning, and (2) this \nevening over the waters around southern MD. However, in both \ncases it looks marginal and brief, so no additional advisories \nhave been issued.\n\nHigh pressure will build overhead this weekend. Winds will vary\na bit from northeast to southeast, but at light speeds less than\n10 knots. Light winds continue Monday as they shift around to \nthe northwest. An increase in north to northeast flow is likely\nin the wake of a backdoor cold front on Tuesday, which may\nprompt the next round of Small Craft Advisories.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nGenerally light but steady northeast winds are expected to keep\nwater levels elevated through the upcoming weekend. When paired\nwith high astronomical tides due to the upcoming full moon on \nOctober 26, additional rounds of minor tidal flooding are likely\nat vulnerable sites like Annapolis, Straits Point, Dahlgren, \nSolomons, and Alexandria. Will have to see if excess water is \nable to slowly escape the mouth of the Chesapeake Bay, or if the\nnortheast flow keeps tides steady or even increases them a bit.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory until 7 PM EDT this evening for MDZ014.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 3 AM EDT early this morning for \n     MDZ016>018.\nVA...Coastal Flood Advisory until 3 AM EDT early this morning for \n     VAZ057.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 11 AM EDT this morning for \n     ANZ530>532-538>540.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT this evening for ANZ533-\n     534-537-541>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF\nAVIATION...DHOF\nMARINE...DHOF\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792740588,"days":{"2026-10-23":{"hi":65,"lo":43,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-24":{"hi":64,"lo":41,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-25":{"hi":67,"lo":39,"pop":0,"short":"Patchy Fog then Sunny"},"2026-10-26":{"hi":70,"lo":49,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":68,"lo":47,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":64,"lo":47,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":68,"lo":null,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792738800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,2.0,2.0,2.0,2.0],"spd":[6,6,7,7,6,6,7,8,8,8